Ecological Chemical Grammar v1.0

Scope

This is a formalism we impose to study chemical ecology. Molecules are not literal human words and not every interaction entails evolved communication. The same molecule can be a hormone, metabolic product, attractant or incidental cue depending on context.

Type system

• CHEM: chemically specified molecule (indole, (Z)-3-hexenol)
• CLASS: chemical class (strigolactones)
• MIXTURE: physically co-present substances (extrafloral_nectar)
• INTERNAL: signal within an organism (glutamate → calcium)
• CUE: exploitable association (inference without establishing sender intent)
• SIGNAL: evolved sender–receiver effect within evidence scope
• ELICITOR: material that induces an organism response (volicitin)
• METABOLITE: compound produced/used in metabolic processing (acetate)
• PASSIVE_SAMPLE: trapped information (spider_web_eDNA)

Proposed EBNF

Observation = Context, Source, Medium, Pattern, Receiver, Response, Evidence ;
Context     = Species, Tissue, Development, Environment, TimeWindow ;
Source      = TaxonOrTissue, [StressOrState], [InteractionPartner] ;
Medium      = "air" | "rhizosphere" | "liquid" | "contact" |
              "internal" | "electrical" | "mechanical" | "passive_deposition" ;
Pattern     = Event, {TemporalRelation, Event} ;
Event       = ChemicalIdentity, Quantity, Position, Timestamp ;
TemporalRelation = "before" | "after" | "overlaps" | "pulses" | "persists" ;
Receiver    = Taxon, DetectionMode, ReceiverState ;
Response    = ActionOrPhysiology, Latency, Magnitude ;
Evidence    = Method, Controls, SourceIDs, Uncertainty, Alternatives ;

This EBNF describes an information record, not an assertion that a plant intentionally constructs sentences.

Grammar operators

|Operator     |Example                               |Why it matters                                             |
|-------------|--------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|
|`IDENTITY`   |indole vs green-leaf volatiles        |Distinguishes pathways                                     |
|`RATIO`      |compound A:B                          |Blends may be discriminatory even when constituents are not|
|`DOSE`       |high vs low emission                  |Thresholds/saturation change effects                       |
|`ORDER`      |damage burst → delayed induction      |May resolve mechanism/stress phase                         |
|`DURATION`   |transient vs persistent release       |Separates acute response from steady background            |
|`LOCATION`   |leaves vs roots                       |Different receivers and transport                          |
|`CONTEXT`    |humid vs dry, day vs night            |Changes transport and physiology                           |
|`RECEIVER`   |parasitoid vs another plant           |One pattern, distinct functional reading                   |
|`FEEDBACK`   |response alters sender/partner        |Makes ecological loops                                     |
|`EAVESDROP`  |hyperparasitoid uses volatile changes |Changes who benefits                                       |
|`NEGATION`   |reliable absence above detection limit|Can exclude hypotheses if sampling supports it             |
|`UNCERTAINTY`|overlap/limited specificity           |Prevents overly literal decoding                           |

Formal primitives

A profile S(t) = [c_1(t),...,c_n(t)] must include units, sampling method, compound identity confidence, detection limit and blanks. Distinguish emission rate from measured concentration because distance, airflow, temperature and sorption change observations.

